included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ . Objective Although meteorological factors are connected with severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome (SFTS) incidence, available findings have been inconsistent. This study was performed to systematically evaluate the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence. Methods We performed a thorough literature search in PubMed, Web of Science, Embase, Cochrane Library, and Chinese databases from databases initiatives to November 30, 2024. Literature was searched for correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence. Two researchers screened the retrieved literature based on exclusion and inclusion criteria. Finally, data extraction and quality evaluation were carried out for the included literature, and meta-analysis was executed applying the R package (4.4.1). Results A total of 404 relevant literature were retrieved, and 12 studies were enrolled in the meta-analysis. Both average temperature ( r s =0.73, 95% CI 0.63–0.81, P <0.001), average relative humidity ( r s =0.46, 95% CI 0.32–0.57, P < 0.001), cumulative precipitation ( r s =0.49, 95% CI 0.33–0.62, P < 0.001), average precipitation ( r s =0.48, 95% CI 0.21–0.68, P < 0.001), and sunlight ( r s =0.34, 95% CI 0.11–0.53, P < 0.01) were positively correlated with SFTS incidence. The average atmospheric pressure was negatively correlated with SFTS incidence ( r s = -0.69, 95% CI -0.78– -0.59, P < 0.001), and the average wind speed was not significantly correlated with SFTS incidence ( P > 0.05). Conclusions Factors such as temperature, humidity, precipitation, sunshine duration, and atmospheric pressure are related to the incidence of SFTS with a certain lag effect. Future studies on the relationship between meteorological factors and the incidence of SFTS should fully consider human activities and environmental factors, and explore the pathogenesis and transmission mechanisms in greater depth, so as to provide targeted preventive measures. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12889-025-21527-8. Keywords Meteorological factors Severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome Emerging infectious disease Meta-analysis Consultancy project by the Chinese Academy of Engineering 2023-JB-12 Key Program of National Natural Science Foundation of China GRANT32090023 pmc-status-qastatus 0 pmc-status-live yes pmc-status-embargo no pmc-status-released yes pmc-prop-open-access yes pmc-prop-olf no pmc-prop-manuscript no pmc-prop-legally-suppressed no pmc-prop-has-pdf yes pmc-prop-has-supplement yes pmc-prop-pdf-only no pmc-prop-suppress-copyright no pmc-prop-is-real-version no pmc-prop-is-scanned-article no pmc-prop-preprint no pmc-prop-in-epmc yes pmc-license-ref CC BY-NC-ND issue-copyright-statement © BioMed Central Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025 Introduction Severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome (SFTS) is an emerging infectious disease that arises from severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome bunyavirus (SFTSV) and was first identified in 2009 [ 1 ]. Early on, SFTS was found to be prevalent mainly in China, but later studies found that it was also prevalent in Asian regions such as Korea, Japan, Vietnam, and Myanmar [ 2 – 5 ]. The cumulative number of reported cases in China from 2018 to 2021 amounted to 8,835, and the distribution is expanding [ 6 ]. It is now recognized that ticks act as a vector for disease transmission, which spreads the virus primarily by attaching itself to human surfaces and biting [ 7 , 8 ]. More notably, transmission from human to human through contact with blood, body fluids, excretions, and their contaminants of SFTS patients [ 9 – 12 ]. Human infections with novel bunyaviruses have an incubation period, with the main symptoms being fever, thrombocytopenia, malaise, and gastrointestinal symptoms, which are similar to clinical symptoms of hematologic system disorders, making it difficult to recognize them as SFTS, and thus delaying the optimal time for treatment [ 13 , 14 ]. This may be a reason for the high mortality rate of SFTS. However, no specific drug or vaccine is available. Hence, the prevention and control of SFTS are particularly important. Failing to do so will result in a serious public health burden. Global climate change is a driver of the spread and prevalence of vector-borne disease(VBD) [ 15 , 16 ]. In recent years, there have been numerous studies of the spatial distribution and seasonal prevalence of the disease in the areas of high SFTS prevalence in China. A thorough understanding of the epidemiological characteristics of SFTS will facilitate disease prevention and control. The distribution of SFTS in China has an obvious spatial correlation and is concentrated in several provinces mainly including Henan, Hubei, Anhui, Shandong, Zhejiang, Jiangsu, and Liaoning [ 6 , 17 , 18 ]. There is a correlation between SFTS prevalence and tick activities in high-prevalence areas [ 19 ]. Meteorological factors are one of the significant factors afflicting tick movement and equally play an essential role in SFTS pathogenesis [ 20 – 22 ], when daylight time is longer than 11 h per day, the reported morbidity rate increases [ 23 ]. The high incidence of SFTS was observed when the average annual temperature was 12.5–17.5 °C and annual relative humidity was 63-82% [ 24 ]. A study in Shandong Province found that areas with SFTS cases had lower temperatures than areas without SFTS cases [ 25 ]. Another study showed the risk of SFTS was higher in temperatures above 20 °C than in cooler weather, and precipitation has less effect on the incidence of SFTS [ 26 ]. Wind speed affects tick growth and reproduction, thus impacting the likelihood of human contact [ 27 ]. Although studies have evaluated the relationships between SFTS incidence and meteorological factors, most of them only assessed the effect of a particular region or a particular meteorological factor, and there are no studies on the correlation between multiple meteorological factors and SFTS incidence in various regions. In addition, there are inconsistent results in the existing studies. To bridge these gaps, The goal of this meta-analysis was to estimate the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence. Methods Observational studies detecting the connection between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence were searched for meta-analysis. We adhere to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines [ 28 ] (Table S1 ) and the presentation of this study by the Guidelines for Reporting Meta-Analyses of Observational Studies in Epidemiology [ 29 ]. The study protocol has been registered on the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (CRD42024500519). Search strategy Two researchers conducted independent searches of PubMed, Embase, Web of Science, Cochrane Library, and Chinese databases from databases initiatives to November 30, 2024. Searching with Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) and free text words related to “severe fever with thrombocytopenia syndrome”, “meteorological factors” and “climate”, the specific search strategy was shown in Table S2 . Selection criteria All original peer-reviewed observational studies (population-based cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional studies) that specifically explored the relevance between SFTS incidence and meteorological factors were included. Case reports, conference abstracts, and reviews were excluded, as well as duplicate publications and literature lacking data and indicators of outcome. Literature selection and data extraction According to the screening criteria, two researchers were involved in the selection procedure: first eliminating duplicates; next, screening of full-text literature based on the criteria for inclusion and exclusion; and finally, selecting the literature to be included in the final analysis by reading the full text, while a third researcher may intervene to make a recommendation or a decision in the event of disagreement. Data were extracted independently from the included literature by the first two researchers using a standardized form, mainly including the literature title, first author and year of publication, study location, duration, sample size, and correlation coefficients between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence. Quality assessment The standard for quality assessment of included studies was adopted from the criteria of the Newcastle - Ottawa Scale (NOS) [ 30 , 31 ] and the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) quality evaluation criteria [ 32 ]. NOS evaluates cohort studies and case-control studies through three modules with eight entries, including selection, comparability, and exposure/outcome. The evaluation of the quality of the literature adopted the semi-quantitative principle of the star system, except for comparability, which could be evaluated with a maximum of 2 stars, the remaining entries could be evaluated with a maximum of 1 star out of a total of 9 stars, and the higher the score suggests the higher the quality of the study. AHRQ quality evaluation criteria for cross-sectional studies, consisting of 11 entries answered with “yes”, “no”, and “unclear”, quality scores are as follows: 0–3, low quality; 4–7, medium quality, 8–11, high quality. The quality of the included literature was assessed independently by the two researchers, and discrepancies were decided upon by discussion, with a third researcher being asked to adjudicate in case of disagreement in the discussion. Statistical analysis Statistical analysis was conducted using the R software package (4.4.1). When selecting a meta-analysis model, if there is substantial evidence (such as similarities in study design, participant characteristics, and interventions) to believe that the treatment effects across different studies do not significantly differ, a fixed-effect model can be chosen. Conversely, when researchers anticipate that the treatment effects may be similar but not identical, a random-effects model is the appropriate choice [ 33 ]. The random effects model estimates the combined effect sizes, which in effect calculates the weighted average of multiple original study effect sizes. The inverse of the sum of the within-study variance and the between-study variance is used as the weights, with adjustments resulting in smaller weights being given to studies with larger sample sizes and larger weights being given to studies with smaller sample sizes. This partially eliminates the effect of heterogeneity [ 34 ]. Subgroup analysis refers to dividing the study population into subgroups according to certain characteristics (e.g., observation unit, different climate types, and the presence of lagged effects, etc.), and then estimating the effect values of the different groups separately and comparing them among subgroups. To detect publication bias, an Egger’s test was conducted; when P > 0.05, it indicates no publication bias. The Pearson correlation coefficients ( r ) from the included literature need to be transformed into Spearman correlation coefficients ( r s ) before meta-analysis according to the following formula [ 35 ]: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
