. 3 ,12 studies described the correlation between average relative humidity and SFTS incidence which indicates that they had a positive association ( r s =0.46, 95% CI 0.32–0.57, P < 0.001). Fig. 3 Forest plot of correlation between average relative humidity and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Relationship between precipitation and the SFTS incidence Our meta-analysis included all studies to assess the correlation between precipitation and SFTS incidence. However, it is important to note that four studies [ 18 , 40 , 41 , 43 ] reported average precipitation and the rest reported cumulative precipitation; therefore, subgroup analyses were performed. Both cumulative ( r s =0.49, 95% CI 0.33–0.62, P < 0.001) (Fig. 4 ) and average precipitation ( r s =0.48, 95% CI 0.21–0.68, P < 0.001) were positively associated with SFTS incidence (Fig. 5 ). Fig. 4 Forest plot of correlation between cumulative precipitation and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Fig. 5 Forest plot of correlation between average precipitation and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Relationship between average atmospheric pressure and SFTS incidence Eight studies were included to assess the correlation between average atmospheric pressure and SFTS incidence. As shown in Fig. 6 , average atmospheric pressure was negatively associated with SFTS incidence ( r s = -0.69, 95% CI -0.78– -0.59, P < 0.001). Fig. 6 Forest plot of correlation between average atmospheric pressure and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Relationship between average wind speed and SFTS incidence Seven studies were included to evaluate the correlation between average wind speed and SFTS incidence. Nevertheless, the difference in correlation between them was not statistically significant ( r s =-0.20, 95% CI -0.43–0.05, P >0.05)(Fig. 7 ). Fig. 7 Forest plot of correlation between average wind speed and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Relationship between sunlight and SFTS incidence In our meta-analysis, sunlight was positively associated with SFTS incidence ( r s =0.34, 95% CI 0.11–0.53, P < 0.01), but the correlation coefficient is less than 0.5, and no strong correlation was found (Fig. 8 ). Fig. 8 Forest plot of correlation between sunlight and SFTS incidence. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Fig. 9 Sensitivity analysis for average precipitation. Note: COR = correlation coefficient; 95% CI = 95% Confidence interval Subgroup analysis Subgroup analyses grouped by observation unit Table 2 displayed the findings of the multiple subgroup analyses regarding the observation unit. In subgroup where monthly is the observation unit, the heterogeneity of the correlation between average relative humidity, average atmospheric pressure and SFTS incidence was significantly reduced. In subgroup where weekly is the observation unit, significantly less variability in the correlation between sunlight and the incidence of SFTS( r s =0.17, P < 0.05). Heterogeneity in the correlation between other meteorological factors and SFTS incidence was not significantly different in subgroups organized by monthly and weekly. In subgroup where daily is the observation unit, the limited number of studies did not allow for heterogeneity values to be derived. Subgroup analyses grouped by climate zone Table 3 showed the subgroup analysis according to climate zones. The climatic zones of the study area are categorized into three types: subtropical, temperate, and mixed climate types. In the subtropical climate subgroup, the heterogeneity in the correlation between average wind speed and SFTS incidence decreased significantly, and the difference in the combined effect sizes was not statistically significant. In the temperate climate subgroup, the heterogeneity between average atmospheric pressure and incidence of SFTS did not vary significantly. In the mixed climate types subgroup, the limited number of studies did not allow for heterogeneity values to be derived. Subgroup analyses grouped by lag structure The results of the subgroup analysis based on the presence or absence of a lag structure were presented in Table 4 . Heterogeneity in the correlation between cumulative precipitation and the incidence of SFTS decreased significantly in the subgroup of the lag structure ( r s =0.49, P < 0.05). Table 2 Subgroup analysis of the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence (by observation unit) Meteorological factors Monthly Weekly Daily No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) Average temperature 9 98% Random 0.79(0.69–0.86)* 4 97% Random 0.63(0.46–0.75)* 1 - Random 0.47(0.46–0.49)* Average relative humidity 7 94% Random 0.59(0.49–0.67)* 4 96% Random 0.21(0.06–0.35)* 1 - Random 0.33(0.31–0.34)* Precipitation Cumulative 6 98% Random 0.60(0.44–0.73)* 4 89% Random 0.28(0.16–0.39)* - - - - Average 3 97% Random 0.52(0.16–0.76)* - - - - 1 - Random 0.48(0.21–0.48)* Average atmospheric pressure 5 90% Random −0.77(−0.83-−0.69)* 3 79% Random −0.54(−0.63-−0.45)* - - - - Average wind speed 4 98% Random −0.30(−0.64−0.12) 3 88% Random −0.07(−0.19−0.05) - - - - Sunlight 6 99% Random 0.39(0.11–0.62)* 2 8% Random 0.17(0.10–0.25)* - - - - Note: * P <0.05; - No relevant data available Table 3 Subgroup analysis of the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence (by climate zone) Meteorological factors Subtropical climate Temperate climate Subtropical climate and temperate climate No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) No. of studies I 2 value Effects model r s (95%CI) Average temperature 7 96% Random 0.71(0.58–0.80)