e various studies of the other meteorological factors did not show a significant reduction in heterogeneity and the correlation coefficients did not change much. Egger’s test showed no significant publication bias except for average temperature ( P >0.05) (Table 5 ). Table 5 Publication bias for each meteorological factors Meteorological factors Egger test t P Average temperature 3.39 <0.05 Average relative humidity 1.51 0.16 Precipitation Cumulative 1.42 0.19 Average 0.81 0.50 Average atmospheric pressure -1.51 0.18 Average wind speed -0.33 0.76 Sunlight 0.62 0.55 Discussion The findings from our meta-analysis provide important insights into the relationship between various meteorological factors and the incidence of SFTS. Understanding these relationships is crucial for predicting and mitigating the impact of SFTS, which is a significant public health concern in endemic regions. The pathways and mechanisms by which meteorological factors influence the incidence of SFTS are complex, with temperature being a crucial factor that is frequently studied. In previous studies, the temperature can also influence the incidence of dengue fever [ 48 , 49 ], hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome [ 50 , 51 ], tuberculosis [ 52 ], scrub typhus [ 53 ], hand, foot and mouth disease [ 54 ], and Ross River virus infection [ 55 ], etc. Many studies have confirmed that the incidence of SFTS shows a seasonal distribution, but there is some variability among regions [ 56 , 57 ]. Our analysis revealed a strong positive correlation ( r s =0.73) between average temperature and SFTS incidence. This is consistent with the known biology of the tick vectors that transmit SFTSV, as higher temperatures favor tick development and population density. For SFTS, on the one hand, the temperature can influence tick growth and reproduction, with suitable temperatures leading to a significant increase in tick density; and on the other hand, that temperature can also influence the survival of SFTSV [ 58 – 61 ]. The appropriate temperature for ticks to live is 20℃ to 24℃, the observed decrease in SFTS risk above 25℃ suggests that while warmer conditions promote tick activity, excessively high temperatures may be detrimental to tick survival or may alter their habitat preferences, leading to a reduced risk of transmission [ 42 ]. The period when ticks reproduce faster is also the period when farmers’ farming activities are more frequent, which undoubtedly increases the chances of ticks attaching to human surfaces to transmit the virus [ 62 ]. Nowadays, global warming has become an important driver of ticks and tick-borne diseases [ 63 ]. This is also strong evidence for the correlation between temperature and SFTS incidence. As the main vector of SFTS, Haemaphysalis longicornis is sensitive to environmental humidity, and prolonged dry periods can shorten tick lifespans [ 64 ]. When relative humidity is at a high level, it increases the oviposition of female ticks, and their oviposition hatchability increases with humidity [ 65 ]. On the other hand, sufficient precipitation, and sunlight exposure also promote vegetation growth and provide a suitable environment for ticks to attach. In our meta-analysis, average relative humidity, precipitation, and sunlight showed a positive correlation with SFTS incidence, but the correlation coefficient was only 0.46, 0.49, and 0.34. This suggests that although the three meteorological factors are associated with the incidence of SFTS, they are still influenced by many other factors [ 66 ] and do not present a strong correlation. Our study found a negative correlation between atmospheric pressure and the incidence of SFTS, which is in line with existing research [ 67 ]. However, existing studies do not elaborate on how atmospheric pressure affects the incidence of SFTS, except for the hypothesis that low atmospheric pressure is more suitable for tick survival. It is worth exploring further. Finally, unlike previous studies, the correlation between wind speed and SFTS incidence in our study was not statistically significant. It is consistent with the findings of a study in Zhejiang Province [ 68 ]. In the subgroup analysis, strong correlations between average temperature and SFTS incidence were found across different observational units, different climatic zones, and subgroups with or without later structures. In addition, the correlation coefficients between average temperature and the incidence of SFTS were higher in the subgroups with monthly observations ( r s = 0.79), in the temperate climate zones ( r s = 0.79), and in the subgroups with a lag structure ( r s = 0.85). This suggests that there is a lagged effect of average temperature on the incidence of SFTS and that a suitable temperature may be more favorable for the survival of ticks [ 69 ]. The presence or absence of a lag structure may be a source of heterogeneity in the combined effect sizes of the correlation between average precipitation and the incidence of SFTS. It may be that precipitation has some long-term effect on both temperature and relative humidity, together, the life cycle of a tick spans from months to years and is greatly influenced by long-term climate changes. They also affect the reproduction, survival, and geographic distribution of ticks, and thus their ability to transmit pathogens [ 70 ]. In recent years, the SFTS-infected areas have been evolving, unsurprisingly, the effects of each meteorological factor on SFTS in different regions were heterogeneous, and the overall ambient temperature had a stronger effect on SFTS incidence than other meteorological factors. This inter-regional variation may be due to factors such as differences in geographic landscapes between regions, differences in human behavior, differences in tick composition, or differences in virus serotypes [ 66 , 71 ]. The Sixth Assessment Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) states that human activities such as land use, deforestation, and urban expansion can affect climate change by causing loss of biodiversity and reducing natural carbon capture and storage, which in turn affects the spread of vector-borne diseases. Of course, the expanding number of SFTS cases has to take into account the point of tick host diversity. The clinical significance of this study lies in several key aspects. First, by identifying strong associations between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence, it provides foundational information for developing predictive models that can help public health authorities forecast potential outbreaks based on weather patterns, enabling timely interventions. Second, understanding the influence of temperature, humidity, and precipitation aids in designing targeted preventive measures; for example, increased surveillance during warmer, more humid periods may be prudent, along with educating communities in high-risk areas about tick protection. Furthermore, the findings emphasize the necessity of integrating meteorological monitoring into public health frameworks, allowing health campaigns to be tailored to alert vulnerable populations during specific climatic conditions that heighten SFTS risk, thereby potentially reducing human exposure to infected ticks. Finally, this study establishes a baseline for future research to explore the biological mechanisms behind these meteorological influences on tick behavior and SFTSV transmission, and understanding these mechanisms will enhance the knowledge base for effectively combating SFTS. The strength of this study lies in its systematic summary of the evidence regarding the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence for the first time. We included studies covering seven high-incidence areas in China and Korea, and searched for the most recent studies and performed a comprehensive systematic assessment based on the latest PRISMA checklist, which explains the correlation between meteorological factors and SFTS incidence more comprehensively. In addition, we included different observation units, climatic zones, and the presence or absence of a lag structure for subgroup analyses to explore the correlation between the six meteorological factors and the incidence of SFTS. Despite a high degree of heterogeneity, sensitivity analyses showed that the study remained robust. There are also some limitations to this study. First, the included studies were all observational studies, which increased the potential for bias compared to RCT studies. However, it is difficult to explore the correlation between SFTS incidence and meteorological factors in a real-world study designed as an RCT trial. Second, we detected substantial heterogeneity despite conducting subgroup analyses. This may be related to the type of studies included. However, due to the lack of data on other factors such as sociodemographic and environmental factors in the included studies, we were unable to obtain and explore and analyze sources of heterogeneity. Third, there are some overlaps in the areas included in the study, which may have led to higher heterogeneity. Fourth, only five wind speed studies were included, which restricts the strength of the evidence on the connection between wind speed and SFTS incidence. Finally, among meteorological factors, temperature and humidity are recognized as important interacting variables that influence disease incidence and progression. This is particularly important when the effect of a single meteorological factor on the incidence of SFTS is not considered. But the present study failed to explore the combined effect of the interaction of different meteorological factors on the incidence of SFTS. Conclusions Current systematic studies and meta-analyses show temperature, humidity, precipitation, sunlight duration, and atmospheric pressure are related to the incidence of SFTS. Relevant departments should strengthen the monitoring of meteorological factors and early warning of SFTS, and remind residents to avoid activities at tick breeding sites during periods of high temperature, high humidity, strong rainfall, and prolonged sunshine. Residents in areas with high incidence should take personal protective measures when going out to work. Overall, meteorological factors undoubtedly have an impact on the incidence and risk of transmission of SFTS, but the mechanisms and pathways involved need to be explored in depth. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 Abbreviations SFTS Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome SFTSV Severe Fever with Thrombocytopenia Syndrome Bunyavirus VBD Vector-Borne Disease PRISMA Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses MeSH Medical Subject Headings NOS Newcastle-Ottawa Scale AHRQ Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality IPCC Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Acknowledgements The authors would like to sincerely thank all the published researchers who provided data for this study. We express our sincere gratitude to Meshack Biwott for his invaluable support in the language revision of this manuscript. Author contributions Guangju Mo: Conceptualization, Methodology, Data curation, Writing-original draft preparation. Hongmei Zhu: Data curation, Writing-review and editing, Supervision. Huaiping Zhu: Writing-review and editing, Supervision. Jing Li: Writing-review and editing, Supervision. Qiyong Liu: Conceptualization, Methodology, Writing-review and editing, Supervision. All authors reviewed the manuscript. Funding This research was funded by grants from consultancy project (2023-JB-12) by the Chinese Academy of Engineering (CAE) and the Key Program of National Natural Science Foundation of China (GRANT32090023). Data availability All data generated or analyzed dur